      title: "Athens: The Law and the Lovers",
      body: `## A wedding and a conquest

The play opens in the palace of **Theseus**, the legendary Duke of Athens, who is to marry **Hippolyta**, queen of the Amazons, in four days, at the new moon. Theseus is impatient: "O, methinks, how slow / This old moon wanes!" Hippolyta is calmer. Then Theseus says something that reminds us how they came to be engaged: "Hippolyta, I wooed thee with my sword, / And won thy love doing thee injuries; / But I will wed thee in another key, / With pomp, with triumph, and with revelling."

Theseus defeated the Amazons in war, and Hippolyta is, in some sense, his captive. The play begins, then, with a marriage that is also a conquest: the masculine order of Athens triumphing over a society of warrior women. Hippolyta says little in this first scene, and directors have played her silence in very different ways—as contented, as resentful, as reserved.

## The law of Athens

Into this scene bursts **Egeus**, an angry father, dragging his daughter **Hermia** and two young men, **Lysander** and **Demetrius**. Egeus wants Hermia to marry Demetrius. Hermia loves Lysander, who, Egeus complains, has "bewitched the bosom of my child" with love songs at her window, locks of his hair, rings, trinkets, and sweets. Egeus demands that the ancient law of Athens be enforced: "As she is mine, I may dispose of her: / Which shall be either to this gentleman / Or to her death, according to our law."

Theseus, the ruler, upholds the law. A father, he tells Hermia, should be to her "as a god," and she is to him "but as a form in wax," which he may shape or destroy. He gives her a choice: obey her father, die, or become a nun, "to live a barren sister all your life, / Chanting faint hymns to the cold fruitless moon." She has until his wedding day to decide.

The comedy, then, begins with the threat of death and the power of fathers over daughters. This is typical of Shakespeare's comedies, which often begin with a harsh law, a tyrannical ruler, a death sentence, or an exile. The comic world must be reached through a dangerous one.

## The course of true love

Left alone, Hermia and Lysander lament their situation. Lysander speaks one of the most famous lines in the play: "The course of true love never did run smooth." The two of them then sing a kind of duet, a catalogue of all the things that can go wrong in love—difference in rank, difference in age, the choice of friends, war, death, sickness—ending in an image of how quickly love is destroyed: "So quick bright things come to confusion."

Then Lysander proposes a plan. He has a rich aunt, a widow, who lives seven leagues from Athens, beyond the reach of its law. They will meet in the wood outside the city the following night, and flee to her house, where they can marry. The wood is the place where, as Lysander says, he once met Hermia and Helena "to do observance to a morn of May."

## Helena

The fourth lover is **Helena**, Hermia's childhood friend. Demetrius once courted Helena, and won her heart; then he abandoned her for Hermia. Helena still loves him desperately. She is tall, Hermia is short; she is fair, Hermia is dark; she feels herself the less attractive of the two, and cannot understand why Demetrius prefers her friend.

Alone, Helena reflects on the irrationality of love in a speech that states one of the play's central themes: "Things base and vile, holding no quantity, / Love can transpose to form and dignity. / Love looks not with the eyes, but with the mind, / And therefore is winged Cupid painted blind." Love does not see what is there; it sees what it imagines. The rest of the play will test this idea in the most extravagant ways.

Then Helena makes a foolish decision. To win Demetrius's gratitude, she will tell him of Hermia's plan to elope, so that he will follow Hermia to the wood—and she will follow him. All four lovers are now headed into the wood.

## The symmetry of the lovers

The four lovers are almost interchangeable. Lysander and Demetrius are both young, well born, and handsome; as Lysander points out, he is "as well derived" and "as well possessed" as Demetrius. Hermia and Helena are childhood friends who grew up "like to a double cherry, seeming parted, / But yet an union in partition." What distinguishes them is not character but whom they love. Love, in this play, is arbitrary: it falls on one person rather than another for no reason that anyone can see.

Some readers find the lovers dull, and wish Shakespeare had given them more distinct personalities. But their sameness is the point. The play is not interested in why Lysander loves Hermia rather than Helena, but in the strange fact that he does, and in how easily that love can be transferred. In the wood, the love-juice of the fairies will expose the arbitrariness of desire with comic precision.

## The date and the occasion

Many scholars have speculated that *A Midsummer Night's Dream* was written for an aristocratic wedding, performed in a great house before the bride and groom, as the mechanicals perform their play before Theseus and Hippolyta. Several weddings of the mid-1590s have been proposed. There is no evidence for any of them, and the title page of the first quarto, printed in 1600, says that the play had been "sundry times publicly acted" by the Lord Chamberlain's Men—that is, it was performed in the public theater. Whatever its origin, it was written to be enjoyed by the paying audience at the Theatre and later at the Globe.

## Shakespeare's sources

As usual, Shakespeare drew on many sources. Theseus and Hippolyta come from Chaucer's *Knight's Tale* and Plutarch's *Life of Theseus*. Pyramus and Thisbe come from **Ovid's *Metamorphoses***, which Shakespeare knew in Latin and in Arthur Golding's English translation of 1567. Oberon, the fairy king, comes from a French romance, *Huon of Bordeaux*. Puck, or **Robin Goodfellow**, comes from English folklore. Bottom's transformation into an ass may owe something to **Apuleius**'s *Golden Ass*, a Roman novel about a man turned into a donkey. But the combination is entirely Shakespeare's own; no single source for the plot has ever been found.

> **Pause and consider.** Helena says that love "looks not with the eyes, but with the mind." Is she describing a weakness of love, or its power?`,
