      body: `## The fairy quarrel

The second act takes us into the **wood**, at night, and into a different world: the kingdom of the fairies. **Puck**, the mischievous servant of the fairy king, meets one of the fairy queen's attendants, and we learn that the king and queen, **Oberon** and **Titania**, are quarreling. Titania has in her keeping a little Indian boy, the son of one of her votaresses, a mortal woman who died in childbirth. Oberon wants the boy as his page; Titania refuses to give him up, out of love for his dead mother.

Then Oberon and Titania themselves enter, from opposite sides, each with a train of followers: "Ill met by moonlight, proud Titania." They accuse each other of infidelity: Titania says that Oberon has loved Hippolyta; Oberon, that Titania has loved Theseus. These are no ordinary fairies, little winged creatures in flowers. They are powerful nature spirits, whose quarrels shake the world.

## The disordered seasons

Titania describes the effects of their quarrel in one of the most famous speeches in the play. Ever since it began, she says, they have not been able to meet to dance their ringlets to the wind, and in revenge the winds have sucked up fogs from the sea, which have fallen on the land as rain, so that rivers have overflowed:

> The ox hath therefore stretched his yoke in vain,
> The ploughman lost his sweat, and the green corn
> Hath rotted ere his youth attained a beard;
> The fold stands empty in the drowned field,
> And crows are fatted with the murrain flock.

The seasons themselves are confused. Frost falls on roses; summer flowers bloom in winter; "the spring, the summer, / The childing autumn, angry winter, change / Their wonted liveries," so that the world cannot tell one from another. "And this same progeny of evils comes / From our debate, from our dissension; / We are their parents and original."

The speech is more than decoration. In the mid-1590s, England suffered a series of disastrously wet summers, with failed harvests, high food prices, hunger, and riots. Shakespeare's audience would have recognized the drowned fields and rotted corn. The fairy quarrel gives these disasters a mythical cause—and suggests a deep connection between the order of nature and the harmony of marriage.

## Love-in-idleness

Oberon decides to punish Titania. He sends Puck to fetch a magic flower, and explains where it came from in a passage of extraordinary beauty. He once saw **Cupid** flying between the moon and the earth, taking aim at "a fair vestal throned by the west," and loosing his arrow. But the arrow was quenched in the chaste beams of the moon,

> And the imperial votaress passed on,
> In maiden meditation, fancy-free.
> Yet marked I where the bolt of Cupid fell.
> It fell upon a little western flower,
> Before milk-white, now purple with love's wound,
> And maidens call it love-in-idleness.

The "fair vestal throned by the west," the virgin queen whom Cupid's arrow cannot touch, is almost certainly a compliment to **Queen Elizabeth**, who never married. The flower, "love-in-idleness," is the wild pansy. Its juice, laid on the eyelids of a sleeper, will make him or her "madly dote / Upon the next live creature that it sees." Oberon will put it on Titania's eyes, so that she will fall in love with some wild beast, and while she is distracted, he will take the boy.

The image of Elizabeth untouched by love, at the very center of a play about the irrational power of desire, is striking. Some modern critics, notably **Louis Montrose** in an influential essay, "Shaping Fantasies" (1983), have read the play as a meditation on the power of a female ruler in a society that expected women to be ruled by men: the Amazon defeated and married, the fairy queen humiliated and subdued, and the virgin queen hovering above it all.

## Oberon and the lovers

While Puck is away, Oberon, invisible, overhears **Demetrius** and **Helena**. Demetrius, searching for Hermia, is furious at Helena for following him. He threatens to leave her to the wild beasts; she declares that she will follow him anyway: "I am your spaniel; and, Demetrius, / The more you beat me, I will fawn on you." The scene is painful, almost humiliating. Oberon, moved, tells Puck to use the flower on the young Athenian man so that he will love Helena. "Thou shalt know the man / By the Athenian garments he hath on."

## Titania's bower

Oberon finds Titania sleeping in her bower, in a place he describes with one of the play's best-known speeches: "I know a bank where the wild thyme blows, / Where oxlips and the nodding violet grows." Her fairies have sung her to sleep with a lullaby, asking that spotted snakes, hedgehogs, newts, and spiders keep away. Oberon squeezes the flower on her eyes, and casts a spell: "What thou seest when thou dost wake, / Do it for thy true-love take."

## Robin Goodfellow

**Puck** is one of Shakespeare's most delightful creations. He is drawn from the English folk figure of **Robin Goodfellow**, a household spirit, or hobgoblin, who was believed to play tricks on people: curdling milk, misleading travelers at night, pulling stools out from under old women, but also doing household chores for those who left out a bowl of cream for him. The fairy who meets Puck in Act 2 lists his tricks, and Puck cheerfully admits them: "I am that merry wanderer of the night."

Puck is mischievous rather than malicious. He loves confusion for its own sake: "Those things do best please me / That befall preposterously." He moves with incredible speed: "I'll put a girdle round about the earth / In forty minutes." He is the spirit of holiday misrule itself, the Lord of Misrule of the fairy world. And he will make the play's most important mistake.

## Fairies and belief

Did Shakespeare's audience believe in fairies? Some certainly did, especially in the countryside. The skeptic **Reginald Scot**, in *The Discoverie of Witchcraft* (1584), mocked the old wives' tales of Robin Goodfellow that had frightened him as a child, and which, he said, were now less believed than before. Shakespeare draws on folk belief but transforms it. His fairies are not dangerous, as in much folklore, where they steal children and lead people to their deaths. They are benevolent, if capricious: at the end of the play, they bless the marriages and the children to come.

> **Pause and consider.** Titania says that the disorder of nature springs from her quarrel with Oberon. What does the play suggest about the connection between love, marriage, and the order of the world?`,
