      title: "Bottom and the Mechanicals",
      body: `## Rude mechanicals

While the lovers and the fairies are tangling in the wood, a third group arrives: six Athenian workmen, whom Puck calls "a crew of patches, rude mechanicals, / That work for bread upon Athenian stalls." They are **Peter Quince**, a carpenter, who has written the play and directs it; **Nick Bottom**, a weaver; **Francis Flute**, a bellows-mender; **Tom Snout**, a tinker; **Snug**, a joiner; and **Robin Starveling**, a tailor. They hope to perform their play at the Duke's wedding and earn a reward. They have come to the wood to rehearse in secret.

Their play is "The most lamentable comedy, and most cruel death of Pyramus and Thisbe," from Ovid's *Metamorphoses*: the story of two young lovers in Babylon, forbidden to marry by their parents, who talk through a crack in the wall between their houses and agree to meet at night at a tomb outside the city. Thisbe arrives first, is frightened by a lion, and flees, dropping her cloak, which the lion bloodies. Pyramus arrives, finds the cloak, believes her dead, and kills himself. Thisbe returns, finds him dead, and kills herself with his sword.

## Bottom

The mechanicals are Shakespeare's affectionate portrait of English workmen—the kind of men who staged mystery plays in the towns, and the kind who stood among the groundlings. They are earnest, anxious, literal-minded, and devoted to their play. The greatest of them is **Bottom**.

Bottom wants to play every part. Cast as Pyramus, the lover, he also wants to play Thisbe ("I'll speak in a monstrous little voice") and the Lion ("I will roar, that I will do any man's heart good to hear me"). When the others worry that a roaring lion will frighten the ladies and get them all hanged, he promises: "I will aggravate my voice so that I will roar you as gently as any sucking dove." He is vain, bossy, and endlessly confident, and he is also generous, good-humored, and utterly without self-doubt.

The mechanicals' great problem is how to stage their play. They worry that the audience will be frightened by the swords and the lion, so they decide to have a prologue explain that Pyramus is not really killed, and that the Lion is really Snug the joiner. They need moonlight, so they decide to have a man represent Moonshine, with a lantern, a bush, and a dog. They need a wall, so they decide to have a man represent Wall, with plaster on him, holding his fingers apart to make the chink.

The joke is on them, but also on the theater itself. Every play depends on the audience's willingness to accept that a man on a bare stage is a king, or a lover, or a lion. The mechanicals, who do not understand this, make visible what every playwright and actor takes for granted.

## Translated

While the mechanicals rehearse, Puck comes upon them, and plays a trick. When Bottom goes offstage for a moment, Puck gives him the **head of an ass**. When he returns, his fellow actors flee in terror. "Bless thee, Bottom, bless thee! Thou art translated." Bottom, who cannot see his own head, thinks they are trying to frighten him, and sings a song to show that he is not afraid.

His singing wakes **Titania**, with the love-juice on her eyes. She falls instantly in love with him: "What angel wakes me from my flowery bed?" She tells him that she loves him, and that she will make him immortal, and gives him four fairies to attend him: Peaseblossom, Cobweb, Moth, and Mustardseed.

The scenes between Titania and Bottom are among the funniest and strangest in Shakespeare. The queen of the fairies, a goddess of nature, dotes on a weaver with an ass's head. And Bottom takes it all in stride. He is courteous to her fairies, asks them to scratch his head and bring him hay, and is not in the least surprised to be loved by a queen. When Titania declares her love, he replies with unexpected wisdom: "Methinks, mistress, you should have little reason for that. And yet, to say the truth, reason and love keep little company together nowadays."

How far the relationship goes is left to the director. Victorian productions made it innocent and pretty; some modern productions, influenced by the Polish critic **Jan Kott**, have made it openly sexual, even grotesque. The text supports a range of readings.

## Oberon relents

Oberon, having got the Indian boy from the doting Titania, takes pity on her. He releases her from the spell; she wakes, sees Bottom, and is horrified: "O, how mine eyes do loathe his visage now!" King and queen are reconciled, and dance together, blessing the coming marriages. The disorder in nature, we understand, is healed.

## Bottom's dream

Puck removes the ass's head, and Bottom wakes alone in the wood, at dawn. He thinks he is still at rehearsal: "When my cue comes, call me, and I will answer." Then he remembers—or almost remembers. His speech is one of the most wonderful moments in Shakespeare:

> I have had a most rare vision. I have had a dream, past the wit of man to say what dream it was. Man is but an ass if he go about to expound this dream. Methought I was—there is no man can tell what. Methought I was—and methought I had—but man is but a patched fool if he will offer to say what methought I had. The eye of man hath not heard, the ear of man hath not seen, man's hand is not able to taste, his tongue to conceive, nor his heart to report, what my dream was.

Bottom's jumbled senses—the eye that hears, the ear that sees—are a comic garbling of a famous passage from **St. Paul**: "Eye hath not seen, nor ear heard, neither have entered into the heart of man, the things which God hath prepared for them that love him" (1 Corinthians 2:9). The weaver, who has been loved by a fairy queen, reaches for the language of mystical revelation to describe an experience beyond words. He will get Peter Quince to write a ballad of it: "It shall be called 'Bottom's Dream,' because it hath no bottom."

The joke is gentle, and it is also profound. Bottom, the least imaginative of men, has had a vision that none of the other characters has had, and the play does not entirely mock it.

> **Pause and consider.** Bottom garbles St. Paul's words about the things God has prepared for those who love him. Is the allusion a mockery of Bottom, a mockery of St. Paul, or something else?`,
